ted Science Learning Chris Walsh Center for Educators and Families of MetroWest Center for Civic Engagement and Service Learning Danforth Art Museum and School at Framingham State Entrepreneur Innovation Center John C. Stalker Institute for Food & Nutrition Mancuso Humanities Workforce Preparation Center MetroWest Economic Research Center MetroWest STEM Education Network Climate & Sustainability Diversity, Inclusion & Community Engagement Framingham State Facts & Figures History Office of the President News & Events Transportation Services University Community Club An AASCU Institution Back Search Explore\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tSearch Back Info For... Explore\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tInfo For... Home About FSU Mancuso Humanities Workforce Preparation Center Humanities & Careers In The News News Archive News Archive The Value of Studying the Humanities “ Society Needs Humanities Majors, ” Gina Barreca, Seattle Times (2/13/2017) “Without Liberal Arts and Sciences, America’s Foundation Crumbles,” Frederick M. Lawrence, The Hill (12/1/2016 ) “For the Sake of Humanity, Teach the Humanities: Liberal Arts Education Is Essential to Good Citizenship,” Jim Haas, Education Week (11/16/2016) “Learning to Be Human: Why Studying the Humanities Is So Vital in the Information Age,” Sophie Gilbert, The Atlantic (6/20/2016) “ What Is the Value of an Education in the Humanities? ” Adam Frank, NPR: Cosmos & Culture (2/2/2016) “Who Needs Philosophers? Scientists, Politicians, and Welders Do,” Douglas MacLean, TIME (11/12/2015) “The Humanities Are an Existentialism, ” Dan Edelstein, Inside Higher Ed (1/21/2014) Career Connections: How the Humanities Can Prepare You for Anything “The World’s Top Economists Just Made the Case for Why We Still Need English Majors,” Heather Long, Washington Post (10/19/2019) “How to Survive in an AI World,” Peg Tyre, Boston Globe (6/13/2019) “To Be a Good Doctor, Study the Humanities,” Angira Patel, Pacific Standard (5/23/2018) “How the Humanities Can Train Entrepreneurs,” Jon Marcus, The Atlantic (9/20/2017) “There’s Still Room for the Humanities in Our Tech-Focused Workforce,” NPR/WNYC: The Takeaway (6/27/2017) “Training Good Doctors Starts with the Liberal Arts,” Lisa V. Adams, Leslie Henderson, and Glenda Shoop, The New Republic (8/15/2016) “Why America’s Business Majors Are in Desperate Need of a Liberal-Arts Education,” Yoni Appelbaum, The Atlantic (6/28/2016) “Humanities and Business Go Hand in Hand,” Tracy Carlson, Boston Globe (4/24/2016) “Building a Bridge Between Engineering and the Humanities,” Julio M. Ottino and Gary Saul Morson, The Chronicle of Higher Education (2/14/2016) “The Unexpected Schools Championing the Liberal Arts,” Jon Marcus, The Atlantic (10/15/2015) “That ‘Useless’ Liberal Arts Degree Has Become Tech’s Hottest Ticket,” George Anders, Forbes (8/17/2015) “Medical School Hopefuls Grapple with Overhauled Entrance Exam,” April Dembosky, NPR: All Things Considered (6/29/2015) Humanities Job Markets & Workforce Trends “The Most Unexpected Workplace Trend Coming in 2020: The Return of the Liberal Arts Major,” Jessica Stillman, Inc.com (11/26/2019) “In the Salary Race, Engineers Spring but English Majors Endure,” David Deming, New York Times (9/20/2019) “Relax, English Majors. You’re Still Plenty Employable,” Goldie Blumenstyk, The Chronicle of Higher Education (7/13/2019) “Employers Embrace Liberal Education,” Hank Reichman, Academe (8/19/2018) “Jobs Will Save the Humanities,” Paul T. Corrigan, The Chronicle of Higher Education (6/28/2018) “The Business Case for Humanities Education,” Heidi Bostik and Ross Gittel, New Hampshire Business Review (9/29/2017) “Surprise! Humanities Degrees Provide a Great Return on Investment,” Jeffrey Dorfman, Forbes (11/20/2014) In this section News Archive News Archive Footer--Navigate Find a Program News & Events Take A Class Request Info Contact Library Directory Careers Facebook Instagram LinkedIn Youtube 100 State Street PO Box 9101 Framingham , MA 01701 United States © 2026